WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.420
Six Moves, from EL Publishing. Move six: grade the content, grow the English.

2
00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:09.127
Here is a real kind of lab conclusion from a level-two student.

3
00:00:09.127 --> 00:00:11.402
The water it was boil at one hundred degree.

4
00:00:11.402 --> 00:00:16.004
The temperature it no change when we add more heat because the energy go to make the gas.

5
00:00:16.454 --> 00:00:22.041
What grade does that get? In a lot of classrooms, a sixty. Spelling, verbs, articles — it bleeds red.

6
00:00:22.041 --> 00:00:28.126
And the student who gets a sixty for that will quit on you by October, and then you've lost the chemistry too.

7
00:00:28.576 --> 00:00:32.569
Look again. Read it as a chemist. Water boils at one hundred degrees.

8
00:00:32.569 --> 00:00:38.241
The temperature stays constant during the phase change. The added energy goes into making the gas.

9
00:00:38.241 --> 00:00:43.218
That is a correct explanation of latent heat. Every point. The English is a level two.

10
00:00:43.218 --> 00:00:45.013
Those are two different things.

11
00:00:45.463 --> 00:00:49.216
The move is two rows on the rubric — or at least two questions in your head.

12
00:00:49.666 --> 00:00:53.627
Row one: content. Did they get the science right? Grade that fully.

13
00:00:53.627 --> 00:00:56.170
This conclusion is full credit for science.

14
00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:01.583
Row two: language. Note it. Give feedback on one thing. Don't let it sink the grade.

15
00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:05.441
This week the one thing is past tense. Boil, boiled. Go, went.

16
00:01:05.441 --> 00:01:12.642
Say it to the student in person; don't just circle it — a circle says you're wrong, the corrected sentence says here's how it goes.

17
00:01:12.642 --> 00:01:17.808
Write the correct sentence above theirs. Next week, a different one thing. The rest will come.

18
00:01:18.258 --> 00:01:20.173
And keep the distinction straight.

19
00:01:20.173 --> 00:01:29.635
An accommodation changes how a student shows what they know: extra time, a word bank, a bilingual dictionary, the question read aloud, a drawing instead of a paragraph.

20
00:01:29.635 --> 00:01:33.240
The standard stays the same. A modification lowers the standard.

21
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:40.618
English learners get accommodations, not modifications — and the ones your student gets are written in their Language Service Plan.

22
00:01:40.618 --> 00:01:41.237
Get a copy.

23
00:01:41.687 --> 00:01:47.606
A student who gets a ninety-five for the science and a note about the verbs comes back tomorrow and writes more.

24
00:01:47.606 --> 00:01:52.574
That's how the English grows: by writing, a lot, without fear, with one thing fixed at a time.

25
00:01:53.024 --> 00:01:58.487
Tomorrow: two rows on the next thing you grade. That's the sixth move, and the series.

26
00:01:58.487 --> 00:02:05.792
All six, with the podcast and the rest of the PD Library, are free at radan55 dot github dot io slash everyteacher.

27
00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:07.062
Thanks for watching.
